  "name": "APCR (ACTIVATED PROTEIN - C RESISTANCE) TEST",
  "code": "1532",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 49.9629%; height: 86.6016px;\" border=\"1\"><colgroup><col style=\"width: 50.0366%;\"><col style=\"width: 50.0366%;\"></colgroup>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18.8672px;\">\n<td><strong>RESULT</strong></td>\n<td><strong>REMARKS</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.8672px;\">\n<td>&gt;=120</td>\n<td>Seconds Negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.8672px;\">\n<td>&lt;120</td>\n<td>Seconds Positive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Note</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This assay is highly sensitive and specific for inherited APC resistance, most commonly due to factor V Leiden&nbsp;mutation.</li>\n<li>Thrombin inhibitors (eg. Hirudin, Argatroban) and low levels (&lt;50%) of factor V may lead to false negative results.</li>\n<li>Aprotinin may lead to false positive results.</li>\n<li>All results should be clinically correlated.</li>\n<li>Test conducted on citrated plasma.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Comment</strong><br>APC resistance (APC-R) is considered one of the most common genetic factors for venous thrombosis. In APC-R the ability of&nbsp;APC to enzymatically degrade factor Va is significantly reduced. The heterozygous defect is associated with a 5 to 10 folds&nbsp;and homozygous defect with 50 to 100 folds increased thrombotic risk. The prevalence in Caucasian population is around&nbsp;4%, but is lower in African &amp; Asian populations. The incidence is 10-40% in thrombophilic population. The estimated&nbsp;thrombotic risk is 1.8 to 2.6 folds in patients with this resistance. Acquired causes of APC resistance are malignancy,<br>Antiphospholipid syndrome, pregnancy and even hospitalization.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
